<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludovica Torelli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1500">1500</a>–<a href="/years/1569">1569</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludovica Torelli was Countess of Guastalla and heiress of the Torelli family. In 1539 she sold Guastalla to Ferrante Gonzaga, a general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, and devoted her fortune to religious works, founding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> the Angelic Sisters of Saint Paul, a congregation linked to the Barnabites. She later established a college for girls in Milan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovica-torelli.jpg" alt="Ludovica Torelli" width="283" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludovica_Torelli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ludovica Torelli was the daughter of Achille Torelli, Count of Guastalla, and Veronica Pallavicini. Her younger brother Francesco died young, leaving her sole heir, and when her father was killed in battle at Luzzara in 1522 she became countess. Her first husband, the Cremonese nobleman Count Ludovico Stanghi, died, as did their young son Achille. In 1525 she married Antonio Martinengo, a violent man eager to control her estates, who was murdered by a brother-in-law on 18 April 1528. Twice widowed, she faced relatives who coveted her lands and urged her into a convent, and the Torelli of Montechiarugolo and Casei repeatedly tried to ambush her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 1530 she lived mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, guided by Battista Carioni da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crema">Crema</a> and Antonio Maria Zaccaria, who became her chaplain. Another branch of the family claimed Guastalla, and the dispute went before Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>; she settled it in October 1539 by selling the county to the condottiero Ferrante Gonzaga, who needed a fortified river port against the French.</p><p>With the proceeds she founded a convent of the Angelic Sisters of Saint Paul and took the name Paola Maria. The Angelicals, meant as the female counterpart of the Barnabite Fathers, went with the Barnabites on their missions, working among women. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> approved the institute, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv">Pope Paul IV</a> imposed the rule of cloister in 1552. Because of her friendship with Gonzaga she was suspected of supporting proto-Protestant groups and called before the Holy Office, which confirmed her status as a laywoman, as she had taken no vows.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She left the convent, keeping control of her property, and in 1557 founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> the Collegio della Guastalla, whose members, the Daughters of Mary, cared for orphaned girls of noble family under Barnabite direction. She supported other religious houses in various parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. She died in 1569 and was buried in the Church of San Fedele in Milan; in 1938, when the college moved to the Villa Pallavicini-Barbò in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a>, her body was placed in its chapel. The college's walled grounds survive as the Giardino della Guastalla, the oldest park in Milan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludovica Torelli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1539 Countess <b>Ludovica Torelli</b> sold Guastalla to Ferrante Gonzaga, general of Charles V and governor of Milan, whose branch of the Gonzaga ruled it from then on.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-guastalla">Duchy of Guastalla</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Guastalla, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.92,10.65&amp;year=1544" aria-label="Guastalla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ludovica Torelli"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5641 3708h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5641 3708h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.92,10.65&amp;year=1544">What was here in 1544? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
